461
00:23:19,447 --> 00:23:22,227
it as a check-the-box exercise
is how it becomes worthless.

462
00:23:22,227 --> 00:23:24,167
So ask a few follow-up questions.

463
00:23:24,727 --> 00:23:28,137
One, which of these fields is
attested versus merely declared?

464
00:23:28,497 --> 00:23:31,857
The next question is like, what's
the level of autonomy and who

465
00:23:31,857 --> 00:23:33,837
in your organization decided it?

466
00:23:34,107 --> 00:23:37,127
This is the element that's often
left out, and it's the one that

467
00:23:37,127 --> 00:23:38,677
determines your blast radius.

468
00:23:38,827 --> 00:23:40,387
What can this thing do without a human?

469
00:23:40,387 --> 00:23:41,607
What credentials does it hold?

470
00:23:41,607 --> 00:23:42,577
What can it reach?

471
00:23:42,907 --> 00:23:46,767
Nobody is gonna hand you that field,
so somebody on your side has to own it.

472
00:23:48,007 --> 00:23:52,257
And then which of the three questions,
does your scanner actually answer?

473
00:23:52,567 --> 00:23:54,357
If you've bought a model scanner, good.

474
00:23:54,397 --> 00:23:54,907
Keep it.

475
00:23:54,997 --> 00:23:58,367
Go and read its scope statement the
way I just read check marks' out.

476
00:23:58,807 --> 00:24:01,647
Find the sentence where it tells
you what it doesn't cover, and

477
00:24:01,647 --> 00:24:04,857
then go look at how that gap is
actually rendered on your dashboard.

478
00:24:06,027 --> 00:24:11,367
And what does your pipeline actually do
with any of this as opposed to store it?

479
00:24:11,927 --> 00:24:15,407
The G7's own conclusion is that the
inventory isn't sufficient until it's

480
00:24:15,407 --> 00:24:17,257
connected to tooling, which we know.

481
00:24:17,467 --> 00:24:21,487
So go and find out which gates
consume which fields and what

482
00:24:21,497 --> 00:24:23,067
happens when a field is absent.

483
00:24:23,077 --> 00:24:26,477
If the answer is it passes, you've
built a very well-documented

484
00:24:26,477 --> 00:24:27,817
version of the problem I described.

485
00:24:28,887 --> 00:24:32,817
The second thing you can do
is keep signing and be precise

486
00:24:32,817 --> 00:24:33,807
about what it buys you.

487
00:24:34,017 --> 00:24:36,507
Don't take any of this as a
reason to lose provenance.

488
00:24:36,507 --> 00:24:39,967
Signing, attestation, and mission
controls are so necessary.

489
00:24:40,217 --> 00:24:41,494
They're cheap now, and they'll…

490
00:24:41,494 --> 00:24:44,017
They're the only reason that
we can say anything at all

491
00:24:44,017 --> 00:24:45,317
about the artifact's origin.

492
00:24:45,837 --> 00:24:49,987
but just stop letting a green check mark
render as a safety verdict in the inter--

493
00:24:50,127 --> 00:24:51,907
interface your engineers actually look at.

494
00:24:51,907 --> 00:24:52,827
So what does this mean?

495
00:24:53,087 --> 00:24:55,187
It means it comes from us,
and it hasn't been altered.

496
00:24:55,217 --> 00:24:56,907
That's all it ever meant.

497
00:24:58,027 --> 00:25:02,677
If you can only invest in one thing now,
in this year, inv-invest in evidence

498
00:25:02,747 --> 00:25:06,867
generated while the thing is running,
what it called, what it reached,

499
00:25:06,877 --> 00:25:08,477
what it was handed, what it emitted.

500
00:25:08,917 --> 00:25:12,087
That's the class of evidence that
covers what build time provenance

501
00:25:12,107 --> 00:25:16,187
structurally cannot, and it's the only
place the behavior exists to be observed.

502
00:25:17,933 --> 00:25:20,873
The third action is to be
honest about the open problems.

503
00:25:21,283 --> 00:25:25,003
And I want to end with this list of
things that I don't think anyone has

504
00:25:25,003 --> 00:25:28,143
solved because I'd rather you hear that
from someone who works on this than

505
00:25:28,143 --> 00:25:30,183
discover it in the pur-procurement cycle.

506
00:25:30,873 --> 00:25:34,213
one, we can't verify most of
what the AIS bomb asserts.

507
00:25:34,213 --> 00:25:37,423
Provenance on training data in
particular is a supplier's word.

508
00:25:37,583 --> 00:25:41,173
There's real cryptographic work underway
to change this, but it's still a gap.

509
00:25:42,303 --> 00:25:45,983
We don't have any agreed,
unit for autonomy.

510
00:25:46,193 --> 00:25:50,063
The G7 group looked straight at it and
concluded that it wasn't ready to be

511
00:25:50,063 --> 00:25:51,733
able to be answered, and they're right.

512
00:25:51,763 --> 00:25:55,213
And until it's ready, the most
dec-decision-relevant property

513
00:25:55,213 --> 00:25:59,343
of the system lives in, prose
and not in anybody's hands, and

514
00:25:59,343 --> 00:26:00,823
definitely not computer readable.

515
00:26:01,633 --> 00:26:05,733
And we don't have a good answer for
what a passing test means when the thing

516
00:26:05,743 --> 00:26:07,943
under the test isn't just deterministic.

517
00:26:08,173 --> 00:26:11,553
Evaluation suites help, but they're not
the same as regression tests, and the

518
00:26:11,553 --> 00:26:16,353
industry is currently pretending harder
than it should that they are the same

519
00:26:17,353 --> 00:26:18,753
I gave you a checklist a minute ago.

520
00:26:18,803 --> 00:26:22,313
If you forget all of it, keep two
of these in your noggin, please.

521
00:26:22,373 --> 00:26:24,193
First, the cheap one
only takes an afternoon.

522
00:26:24,253 --> 00:26:28,303
I told you to ask your scanner which of
the three questions it actually answers.

523
00:26:28,353 --> 00:26:30,673
Don't, don't obviously
just don't ask it, test it.

524
00:26:30,683 --> 00:26:32,733
Point it at an image and
watch what comes back.

525
00:26:33,063 --> 00:26:36,223
Not whether it says pass, but whether
it's reading the thing at all.

526
00:26:36,253 --> 00:26:39,093
In most organizations,
that has not been checked.

527
00:26:39,443 --> 00:26:42,093
Not out of negligence, but because
the tool has been green the whole

528
00:26:42,093 --> 00:26:45,343
time, and green is also what it looks
like when a control isn't there.

529
00:26:46,223 --> 00:26:47,863
Second, the real one.

530
00:26:48,773 --> 00:26:52,823
Take the autonomy question I gave
you, the one the G7 left out, and

531
00:26:52,823 --> 00:26:54,333
make somebody answer it in writing.

532
00:26:54,443 --> 00:26:56,833
Write that risk acceptance,
a name, and a date.

533
00:26:57,483 --> 00:27:00,923
And I know how this can land in
a DevSecOps room, but I promise

534
00:27:00,923 --> 00:27:02,013
you it's not just paperwork.

535
00:27:02,303 --> 00:27:06,073
You cannot write that document
without also asking what the thing

536
00:27:06,083 --> 00:27:07,873
is actually allowed to do on its own.

537
00:27:08,003 --> 00:27:10,023
Getting it documented is really important.

538
00:27:10,063 --> 00:27:12,733
What it can reach, what
it holds, who decides?

539
00:27:13,243 --> 00:27:15,003
That's the field nobody hands you.

540
00:27:15,003 --> 00:27:19,063
And in most organizations, the person
who owns that field hasn't been named.

541
00:27:19,193 --> 00:27:21,923
So writing it down gets that
risk highlighted, which is

542
00:27:21,923 --> 00:27:23,103
the whole point of the ask.

543
00:27:23,903 --> 00:27:28,423
Because here's the day this matters, that
something goes wrong with one of these.

544
00:27:28,483 --> 00:27:31,373
It emits something it shouldn't
have, or takes an action that nobody

545
00:27:31,383 --> 00:27:34,433
sanctioned, or turns out to have been
trained on something you can't license.

546
00:27:34,643 --> 00:27:37,833
And the question you'll be asked
by your CISO or your board or a

547
00:27:37,833 --> 00:27:41,843
regulator is the question that always
gets asked, which is, "What did

548
00:27:41,843 --> 00:27:44,573
we know, and when did we know it?"

549
00:27:45,133 --> 00:27:48,383
Right now in most organizations, the
honest answer is our tooling said

550
00:27:48,383 --> 00:27:50,993
green, and nobody ever asked if it
looked at the thing in question.

551
00:27:50,993 --> 00:27:52,403
We just assumed that it did.

552
00:27:53,863 --> 00:27:56,613
I'd like that to be a better answer
than it currently is, and the only

553
00:27:56,613 --> 00:27:59,373
thing standing between you and
a better one is a decision that

554
00:27:59,373 --> 00:28:00,703
nobody has been asked to make yet.

555
00:28:00,843 --> 00:28:02,633
So go ahead and make that
decision with your team.

556
00:28:03,683 --> 00:28:04,153
Thank you.

557
00:28:04,343 --> 00:28:05,163
I'm Emmy Eide.

558
00:28:05,263 --> 00:28:07,873
I'm on LinkedIn, and I write
about this stuff most weeks.

559
00:28:08,223 --> 00:28:11,703
If you go and run that check and find
something surprising, I genuinely

560
00:28:11,713 --> 00:28:14,293
would like to hear about it and
enjoy the rest of the conference.

